# Line endings are a property of the REPO, not of whoever cloned it.
#
# Without this file, git falls back to each machine's core.autocrlf. On Windows
# that is typically `true`, which asks for CRLF in the working tree — but every
# tool here (the formatter, the bundler, editors) writes LF, so ~200 files sat
# permanently "modified" with no content difference, burying the files that had
# actually changed and making `git add -A` a hazard.
#
# LF everywhere, declared once. This matches what git already stores, so it
# rewrites nothing; it only stops git expecting something else on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST be LF regardless of platform. docker-entrypoint.sh runs as
# the container's ENTRYPOINT under Alpine's /bin/sh: a CRLF checkout makes the
# kernel read the shebang as "/bin/sh\r" and the container dies with a bare
# "no such file or directory" that names a file which plainly exists.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf

# Never touched, whatever they contain.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.wasm binary
pnpm-lock.yaml -diff linguist-generated
